#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985} :activity/id #uuid "1bda195a-6aa9-5403-8c13-a26311503b1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/math-academy-id 3344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "8de1d301-7c80-5374-bd47-c7e3b3821626" :step/math-academy-id 35366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890 :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "7533639d-bfe8-58f0-b64f-5c767246da78" :step/math-academy-id 35367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173197 :tutorial/content "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ assigns a vector to every point $(x,y)$ in a region $S\\subset \\mathbb R^2$. We can visualize this by plotting the corresponding vector at each point in the coordinate plane.\n\nAs an example, let's sketch the vector field $\\mathbf F$ at the points $(1,1), (-3,3),$ and $(4,-3),$ where $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j}.$$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we first substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =i+j \\\\ F(-3,3) & =-3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ F(4,-3) & =4\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors, placing the tail ends of each vector at its corresponding point. This gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/da/daea999f1a9bff587f0550e6674a39ae27dc9a2bb58e35877ed15626de0d9791.png)\n\nApplying the same method to more points in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we obtain the following visualization of our vector field:\n\n![](images/03/03402a7a7e1967b428c3657d6be90e4b1fd7ddd3c8ccaa11a69b0ff7cd566859.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ed5e3d1-f4e2-5773-bbd0-5ef02d8b726d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f845bde7-8e1b-5e89-8f3e-66a8c7e912eb" :step/math-academy-id 35369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "64cb83b5-85c0-5f79-8253-5fd52adedcd8" :step/math-academy-id 35450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173198 :tutorial/content "Given a visualization, it's possible to determine characteristics about the underlying vector field.\n\nFor example, let's consider the visualization below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e3ecb8b2d64aef1cf4654fea8e3ad8f176e9d830ee8b402a281155c3b4ef645.png)\n\nThis visualization was generated by one of the following functions.\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\nWe can determine which function generated the above diagram using a process of elimination:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. This means we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf j$ component is negative for some $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. This means that the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is dependent on the variable $x.$\n\nSo, we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf i$ component is independent of $x.$ Hence, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf H(x,y) = y\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372dc560-67d1-5888-9353-8f8f365cff15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5773 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Vector Field Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "51e7b312-b65a-5a9c-88d2-2bb1e9411b96" :step/math-academy-id 35467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172171}]}
